WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.830
Of all the actors who came of age in the '80s,
few seemed as destined for superstardom as

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.900
Rebecca De Mornay.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.180
Her wit, humanity, and charisma seemed to
put her a cut above her contemporaries, but

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.200
where did her career end up taking her?

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.359
While she never reached superstar status,
you've probably seen more of her than you

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:16.940
realize.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.140
De Mornay got her foot in the door of Hollywood
with a minor role in 1981's One From the Heart

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.900
— a musical romance from director Francis
Ford Coppola.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.109
If you haven't heard of it, it's probably
because it hasn't held on in pop culture like

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:33.450
some of the director's other movies, such
as The Godfather, Apocalypse Now, or…

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.450
Jack.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:38.550
But it was still a strong start for De Mornay,
who followed up her debut with a breakout

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.280
lead role in Risky Business.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.000
That movie, a legit '80s classic, practically
made an icon out of her co-star, fellow up-and-comer

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.440
Tom Cruise.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.800
If you're totally unfamiliar, Risky Business
is a classic coming-of-age tale where boy

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:57.030
meets girl… except the girl is a mysterious
prostitute and the boy is an inexperienced

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:57.660
suburbanite.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.000
The two meet when Cruise's questionable decision
making skills land him in serious financial

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.520
straits, forcing him to find a unique way
to make a lot of money in a short amount of

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.520
time.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.040
The result is a comedy about a homegrown brothel
that somehow stays charming, despite the sleaze.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.520
The movie made Cruise an overnight superstar
and marked De Mornay as an instant "it girl."

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.600
But while Cruise's star power continued to
grow, De Mornay's new status proved a little

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.960
bit harder to hold on to.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.240
After Risky Business, De Mornay's career took
off.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.259
She followed her breakout film with a supporting
turn in 1983's Testament, but her stardom

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:34.299
took a serious hit in 1985 with the poorly-reviewed
baseball rom-com The Slugger's Wife.

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:38.000
You can't blame her for jumping onto a film
written by Neil Simon and directed by Hal

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.240
Ashby it just didn't work out.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.880
Luckily, De Mornay kept her footing with a
pair of solid supporting turns in 1985.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.200
The first — a heartfelt drama called The
Trip to Bountiful — found her sharing scenes

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.360
with screen legend Geraldine Page in a movie
that got attention from the Oscars.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.409
The other, Runaway Train, is more of a pulse-pounding
crowd-pleaser, with the actress as a railroad

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:02.360
employee working to thwart two escaped convicts
that have sabotaged a train.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.159
Despite having a story by Akira Kurosawa and
earning three Oscar nominations, the movie

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.520
is now often overlooked.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.420
But it was impressive at the time, and helped
keep De Mornay's career chugging right along.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.160
In 1986, De Mornay caught a ride with the
rock band Starship.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.260
Specifically, she co-starred in the music
video for "Sara,"

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.260
The song was one of the most successful tracks
of the year, climbing the charts to hit number

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.940
one on the Billboard Hot 100.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.540
De Mornay appeared in the video for the shamelessly
schmaltzy song alongside Starship singer Mickey

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.180
Thomas, telling a story about a dissolving
relationship.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.280
Despite its contemporary success, the song
doesn't get much play anymore, but at least

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.540
it hasn't become as hated as "We Built This
City" did.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.280
Though the 80s started strong for her, De
Mornay spent the second half of the decade

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.850
starring in projects that ranged from questionable
to downright abominable.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:56.150
These movies included And God Created Woman,
Feds, and Dealers, but the worst one was 1987's

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.040
mind-bogglingly bad version of Beauty and
the Beast.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.319
On paper, it doesn't sound so bad.

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:05.860
The movie was one of only a few English-language
adaptations of the classic fairy tale, and

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.930
the first to come up with the idea of turning
it into a musical, beating Disney to the concept

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.180
by a full five years.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.540
John Savage was cast to play the Beast to
De Mornay's Beauty, but neither was served

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.209
well by the finished product.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:21.640
The movie was criticized for being half-hearted
and uninspired, with less-than-stellar make-up

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.689
effects and unmemorable music.

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:27.299
The actors deserve credit for performing the
songs themselves, but it was ultimately a

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:28.780
risk that didn't pay off.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:29.780
Hitting a backdraft, rocking the cradle

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.879
After a string of cinematic misfires, De Mornay
hit the reset button on her career.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.980
The actress kicked off the 90s with a small-screen
role as a bomber pilot in HBO's Emmy-winning,

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.820
Cold War-tinged HBO original By Dawn's Early
Light.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.420
The next year, in 1991, she found herself
at the top of the box office when Ron Howard

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.360
cast her in his smash hit firefighter drama
Backdraft.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.450
While De Mornay's role is only a supporting
one, her skills on the big screen still showed.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.499
It's a performance that must have made an
impression with her peers, particularly director

00:03:58.499 --> 00:04:03.739
Curtis Hanson, who cast her as a villainous
would-be nanny in his 1992 thriller The Hand

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.820
That Rocks the Cradle.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:09.360
De Mornay more than delivered the goods as
the psychologically-damaged character, and

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.019
this time found herself the lead in a blockbuster
hit that held the number one spot at the box

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.140
office for four straight weeks.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.750
De Mornay spent the '90s coupling her acting
projects with producer credits, with work

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.140
including Never Talk To Strangers, The Winner,
and A Table For One.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:29.080
De Mornay also made an appearance on the mid-'90s
revival of the popular anthology series The

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.700
Outer Limits, on which she earned her one
and only credit as a director.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.300
Her episode, "The Conversion," premiered as
the 12th installment of the series, telling

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.000
a complex, violent story.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.460
"The Conversion" follows a man fresh out of
prison and harboring a serious grudge against

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.440
his former employers — the sort of grudge
that drives him to pick up a gun and open

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.800
fire on his old office's Christmas party.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.480
As one might expect with the sci-fi/fantasy
series, all is not what it appears to be,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.380
with De Mornay playing a key role as a mysterious
woman who may or may not be pulling the strings

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:00.380
on the whole situation.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.380
Well what were you thinking about, buying
a drink for someone you don't even know?"

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.060
"Maybe I feel like I know you."

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.320
Working behind the camera, she handled the
challenging material with style and grit,

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.520
delivering a violent, clever, character study
about mistakes and second chances.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.220
She also got to direct her old Beauty and
the Beast co-star John Savage in the process.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.840
Rebecca De Mornay's career continued to flourish
throughout the '90s, but 1997 saw the actor

00:05:26.851 --> 00:05:31.940
take one of her biggest risks yet when she
landed the role of Wendy Torrance in the made-for-TV

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.700
miniseries adaptation of Stephen King's The
Shining.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.400
The story had already been adapted by Stanley
Kubrick almost two decades prior, with iconic

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.800
performances from Jack Nicholson and Shelley
Duvall.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.600
With the original being regarded by many as
one of the best horror movies ever made, the

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:51.020
stakes were high for the three-part miniseries,
with comparisons to the classic first adaptation

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.130
being inevitable.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.980
King, being no fan of Kubrick's adaptation,
took on the task of scripting the episodes

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:01.240
himself, resulting in a four-and-a-half-hour
adaptation that's as faithful to his novel

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.480
as possible — for better or for worse.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.900
As for De Mornay and her on-screen husband,
Steven Weber, each finds a way to make the

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.129
characters their own, helping make their version
of The Shining a respectable effort that has

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:13.900
Stephen King's seal of approval.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.800
After finding some genuine small-screen success
with the Emmy-nominated adaptation of The

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:22.360
Shining, De Mornay wasted little time booking
her next TV gig.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.090
She ended up landing on one of the greatest
and longest-running series ever produced:

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:27.600
ER.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.500
De Mornay made her debut in the first episode
of the series' sixth season, playing Elaine

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:37.560
Nichols, a wealthy seductress in a relationship
with Noah Wyle's Dr. Carter, with the twist

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.780
being that her character is also the recently-divorced
ex-wife of Carter's cousin.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.479
"What was it you said?

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.180
'I'll be thinking of you tonight?'"

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.200
"I guess I meant it."

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.319
Throughout De Mornay's arc, the pair face
numerous challenges, including the obvious

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:56.199
family turmoil, their dramatic age difference,
and eventually a breast cancer storyline that

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.440
sees Carter trying to be supportive as Elaine
endures a mastectomy.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.060
Eventually, the couple ends things with Elaine
running off to Europe to start anew, and Carter

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.440
staying in Chicago to continue saving lives.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.220
Though De Mornay's role lasted just five episodes,
it remains one of the series' more memorable

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.220
guest appearances.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:16.280
Though she's had serious successes on the
small screen, De Mornay has always done her

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:17.740
best work in feature films.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.940
A shrewd bit of casting gave De Mornay a chance
to showcase the full range of her talents

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:27.210
when she played the role of an aging TV star
with a nasty streak in James Mangold's psychological

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.160
thriller Identity.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:33.080
Conceived as an Agatha Christie-style whodunnit,
the movie is an ensemble piece following a

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:37.080
group of strangers being stalked by a murderer
in a seedy, isolated motel.

00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:41.460
At the same time, the movie focuses on the
testimony of a potentially schizophrenic death

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.940
row inmate, whose mind may hold the key to
the gruesome mystery at hand.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:50.069
Needless to say, there's a lot to unpack with
Identity as the film twists and turns to an

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.400
unexpected conclusion.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:56.020
Over 15 years after its release, the movie
remains a uniquely satisfying mindbender,

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:00.140
that just so happens to feature one of the
best performances of Rebecca De Mornay's career.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.960
Though she was blessed with leading lady looks
and acting chops to match, De Mornay has never

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.980
been one to shy away from playing compelling
supporting characters in bigger movies.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:12.830
More often than not, she's made the most of
these minor roles, bringing rich personalities

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.990
to even the most marginal of characters.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:19.759
Of her supporting turns, few were more memorable
— or more brief — than her role as a vindictive

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.580
divorcee in the opening moments of 2005's
outlandish comedy Wedding Crashers.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:29.340
She's the woman who traded barbs with Dwight
Yoakam as one half of a soon-to-be-divorced

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.599
couple, who are forced to remember the good
times by Vince Vaughn and Owen Wilson's divorce

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:34.400
mediators.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:38.260
Though the scene clocks in at just about three
and a half minutes, it serves as a perfect

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:42.640
tone-setter for the raucous action that follows,
all mostly thanks to De Mornay.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.460
You SHUT your mouth when you're talking to
me."

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:50.520
Now more than four decades into her career,
De Mornay's professional life has continued

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:51.280
to flourish.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.500
Like many former "it girls" and young movie
icons who came up exploiting their sex appeal,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.700
De Mornay has made a second career of sorts
out of playing moms on screen.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:03.000
In the last 15 years alone, she's played a
parental role in over a dozen projects on

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.230
screens both big and small, including turns
on John From Cincinnati, American Reunion,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:13.000
Mother's Day, American Venus, and Hatfields
&amp; McCoys, all while playing real-life mom

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.240
to two daughters of her own.

00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:18.519
Of course, De Mornay's most memorable turn
as a fictitious mother has been as Dorothy

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.440
Walker, the fame-hungry woman whose daughter
is Trish "Patsy" Walker on Netflix's Jessica

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:24.160
Jones.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.440
Like many of her supporting turns over the
years, De Mornay brings a hefty dose of humanity

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.870
to the role, helping make a complex human
being out of what might otherwise have been

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:34.760
a shrill, patently unlikable character.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.020
"Taking you in was the worst decision of my
life."

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:39.640
"Thanks, Mom."

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.740
As Rebecca De Mornay nears her 60th birthday,
it's clear that the acclaimed actor has no

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:50.839
intention of slowing down, having recently
completed a two-episode arc on Fox's Lucifer

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.760
and appearing in 2018's family drama Periphery.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.120
As for what lies ahead, we expect to see De
Mornay reprise her role as Dorothy Walker

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.020
in the third season of Jessica Jones.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:04.620
She's also slated to play a key role in the
Nick Cannon-directed women's basketball drama

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.180
She Ball.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:09.280
From there, the sky seems to be the limit
for the gifted career performer.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.300
One thing we know for sure is that she seems
primed to continue playing strong, intelligent,

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.340
complex women on TV shows and movies for years
to come.

